  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "In a right triangle $△LMN,$ $∠M$ is a right angle, $MN=\\sqrt{5}$ and $LM=\\sqrt{11}.$ Determine the cosine of $∠N.$",
  "worked_solution": "The following triangle illustrates the information given.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14224552/assets/df7dcd343dd2676cb38b7df3c2d8cb599fed45b9c0b25194609effe2bf27a6f7.png)\n\nRecall the definition of cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡N=\\frac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{MN}{LN}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{LN}\n$$\n\nHere, we need to determine the missing length $LN.$ The trick is to use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}LN & =\\sqrt{M{N}^{2}+L{M}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{5})^{2}+(\\sqrt{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5+11} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡N & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{LN}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
